**What and Why:**
Instance methods = methods that work on one specific object's data using `self`. You've been doing this since Day 1 — today's just the formal name. New things today: a method calling another method inside same class, and understanding `return` vs `print` properly. This is critical — getting this wrong causes real bugs in Django views later.

---

```python
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 1: INSTANCE METHODS AND RETURN VS PRINT
# ============================================================

class Student:
    def __init__(self, name, marks):
        self.name = name
        self.marks = marks
        # marks = list of numbers — example: [80, 45, 60]
        # one student can have marks for multiple subjects
        # list = [80, 45, 60] means 3 subjects, scores 80, 45, 60

    def average(self):
        # sum() = built-in Python function, adds all numbers in list
        # sum([80, 45, 60]) = 185
        # len() = built-in Python function, counts items in list
        # len([80, 45, 60]) = 3
        # 185 / 3 = 61.6
        return sum(self.marks) / len(self.marks)
        # RETURN not print — because result() needs this number
        # if we print here, value disappears — result() gets None, crashes

    def result(self):
        avg = self.average()
        # calling another method inside same class using self.methodname()
        # avg now holds the number returned by average()
        # this is normal — methods can call other methods on same object

        if avg >= 40:
            return "Pass"
        else:
            return "Fail"

    def grade(self):
        avg = self.average()
        # reusing average() instead of recalculating
        # this is good practice — never repeat same logic twice

        if avg >= 75:
            return "A"
        elif avg >= 60:
            return "B"
        elif avg >= 40:
            return "C"
        else:
            return "F"  # below 40

    def highest_mark(self):
        return max(self.marks)
        # max() = built-in Python function
        # finds highest number in list automatically
        # max([80, 45, 60]) = 80

    def lowest_mark(self):
        return min(self.marks)
        # min() = built-in Python function
        # finds lowest number in list
        # min([80, 45, 60]) = 45


# ============================================================
# CREATING OBJECTS AND TESTING
# ============================================================

s1 = Student("Ravi", [80, 45, 60])
s2 = Student("Sai", [30, 25, 35])

print(s1.name, "->", s1.result(), s1.grade(), s1.highest_mark(), s1.lowest_mark())
# Ravi -> Pass B 80 45

print(s2.name, "->", s2.result(), s2.grade(), s2.highest_mark(), s2.lowest_mark())
# Sai -> Fail F 35 25
```

